GOVERNMENT OF INDIA 
MINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION 
Lok Sabha 
UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 705 
 ( TO BE ANSWERED ON THE  24th July 2025 )  
DGCA SAFETY AUDIT 
705.    SHRI DHAIRYASHEEL SAMABHAJIRAO MANE      
             SHRI CHAVAN RAVINDRA VASANTRAO           
             SMT  JUNE MALIAH                        
              MR PATHAN YUSUF                     
             SHRI SUDHEER GUPTA                       
             SHRI MANISH JAISWAL                      
Will the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION 
be pleased to state:- 
(a) whether the Directorate General of Civil Aviation (DGCA) has 
conducted any safety inspections as per the conventions of 
international civil aviation norms recently across various airports in 
the country; 

(b) if so, the details of such ins pections carried out during the last 
two years and the current year and the findings thereof, year-wise; 

(c) whether the DGCA has found any compliance issues by some 
airline companies during the said safety inspections and if so, the 
details thereof along with the names of such airline companies; 

(d) whether the Government has issued any directives to such 
airline companies which are not adhering to the conventions of 
international civil aviation norms; 

(e) if so, the details thereof, airline company -wise along with the 
response of the said airline companies in this regard; and 

(f) the other measures being taken to improve airworthiness, pilot 
training and compliance of guidelines by airlines? 

ANSWER 
Minister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION      
(Shri Murlidhar Mohol) 
(a) to (f): Directorate General of Civil Aviation (DGCA) has a 
comprehensive and structured  civil aviation regulations for safe 
operation of aircraft and its maintenance. These regulations are 
continuously updated and aligned wit h the International Civil 
Aviation Organisation (ICAO)/European Union Aviation Safety 
Agency (EASA) standards. 
; 
DGCA also has a structured surveillance and audit framework in 
place i.e. planned and unplanned surveillance of organization 
/aircraft, which i ncludes regular and periodic audits, spot checks, 
night surveillance and ramp inspections across all operators, 
including continuous oversight of maintenance practices. In case 
there is a violation, DGCA takes enforcement action as per its 
Enforcement Policy and Procedure Manual.; 
; 
DGCA conducted comprehensive safety audit recently at five 
airports i.e. Delhi, Mumbai, Kolkata, Patna and Hyderabad. 
Scheduled Airlines were also covered during these inspections.; 
; 
Following areas were covered during these inspections:-; 
1. Aircraft Maintenance and Airworthiness; 
2. Ramp Inspection; 
3. Breath Analyser Test; 
4. Ground Handling; 
5. Air Traffic Management and Cabin Communication, Navigation 
and Surveillance (CNS) (Air Navigation Services Standards - ANSS); 
6. Cabin Safety; 
7. Aerodrome and Airside Operations; 
8. State Govt-Civil Aviation Directorate (CAD); 
; 
Details of inspection carried out during the last two years and the 
current year including Ramp, Safety Oversight of Foreign Airlines 
(SOFA), Spot Checks and Night Checks, are as follows:-; 

Year;              No of Inspections;      Enforcement ; 
                                                          Action; 
2023;               5745;                           542; 
2024;               6009;                           673; 
2025 ;              1999                           254; 
(Till April); 
; 
Findings recorded during these inspections were forwarded to the 
concerned organisations for taking necessary corrective actions.;                  
; 
Pilot training is as per extant regulations as specified under various 
Civil Aviation Requirements (CARs) and Operations circulars which 
are inline with the International Civil Aviation Organisation (ICAO) 
and global best practices. 
